{"id":"food","name":"Food","aliases":[],"sources":["nave","easton"],"topics":["nave:food"],"groups":["Articles Of"],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Food","text":"Originally the Creator granted the use of the vegetable world for food to man (Gen. 1:29), with the exception mentioned (2:17). The use of animal food was probably not unknown to the antediluvians. There is, however, a distinct law on the subject given to Noah after the Deluge (Gen. 9:2-5). Various articles of food used in the patriarchal age are mentioned in Gen. 18:6-8; 25:34; 27:3, 4; 43:11. Regarding the food of the Israelites in Egypt, see Ex. 16:3; Num. 11:5. In the wilderness their ordinary food was miraculously supplied in the manna. They had also quails (Ex. 16:11-13; Num. 11:31). In the law of Moses there are special regulations as to the animals to be used for food (Lev. 11; Deut. 14:3-21). The Jews were also forbidden to use as food anything that had been consecrated to idols (Ex. 34:15), or animals that had died of disease or had been torn by wild beasts (Ex. 22:31; Lev. …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H2930","lemma":"טָמֵא","xlit":"ṭâmêʼ","gloss":"to be foul, especially in a ceremial or moral sense","kjv":"defile, pollute, be  unclean, utterly","count":16,"share":0.15},{"strong":"H5038","lemma":"נְבֵלָה","xlit":"nᵉbêlâh","gloss":"a flabby thing","kjv":"body, carcase, dead of itself, which died","count":13,"share":0.12},{"strong":"H2931","lemma":"טָמֵא","xlit":"ṭâmêʼ","gloss":"foul in a religious sense","kjv":"defiled, infamous, polluted, unclean","count":13,"share":0.12},{"strong":"H8318","lemma":"שֶׁרֶץ","xlit":"sherets","gloss":"a swarm","kjv":"creep, move","count":10,"share":0.09},{"strong":"H3899","lemma":"לֶחֶם","xlit":"lechem","gloss":"food , especially bread, or grain","kjv":"bread, eat, food, fruit","count":14,"share":0.13},{"strong":"H8263","lemma":"שֶׁקֶץ","xlit":"sheqets","gloss":"filth","kjv":"abominable","count":8,"share":0.07}],"verses":["Lev.11.24","Lev.11.25","Lev.11.26","Lev.